# Line endings are LF everywhere, on every platform — shell scripts
# break under CRLF and the toolchain (rustfmt, prettier) emits LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only files that genuinely require CRLF would be listed here;
# none exist today.

# Wire fixtures are stored as the byte sequence they stand in for, never
# normalised. An MT940 file is a CRLF format, and a golden file whose line
# endings git rewrote would stop being the thing the reader is tested against.
platform/alo-store/tests/fixtures/bank/*.sta -text
# The same holds for a bank's CSV export: one of these is Windows-1252 with
# CRLF, which is what half of Europe's portals write, and normalising it would
# quietly delete the encoding fallback the reader is tested on.
platform/alo-store/tests/fixtures/bank/*.csv -text
